LOCATION:Auer Hall
SUMMARY:Émile Naoumoff\, piano
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  Beethoven: Sonata in F Minor\, Op. 2 No. 1: (1795
 )Chopin: Mazurka in F Minor\, Op. 68 No. 4 (1849)Chopin: Waltz in C-Sharp
  Minor\, Op. 64 No. 2 (1847)Chopin: Nocturne in D-Flat Major\, Op. 27 No.
  2 (1836)Chopin: Prelude in -Flat Major\, Op. 28 No. 15 (c.1839)Chopin: E
 tude in E-Flat Minor\, Op. 10 No. 6 (1832)Rachmaninoff: Étude-tableau in
  E-Flat Minor\, Op. 39: No. 5 (1917)Scriabin: Etude in C-Sharp Minor\, Op
 . 2 No. 1 (1887)Brahms: Intermezzo in A Major\, Op. 118 No. 2 (1893)Naoum
 off: Romance in D Minor (2012)About the ArtistÉmileNaoumoff is professor
  of music in piano at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Music\, whe
 re he has been on faculty since 1998. He has been likened to both Vladimi
 r Horowitz and Arthur Rubinstein as a pianist\, displaying\, as one criti
 c remarked\, the fire of the former and the poetry of the latter. He reve
 aled himself as a musical prodigy at age five\, playing the piano and add
 ing composition to his studies a year later. At age seven\, he became the
  last disciple of Nadia Boulanger\, who referred to him as "the gift of m
 y old age." At age 18\, he was signed by music publisher Schott\, Mainz. 
 Naoumoff studied with Boulanger until her death\, permitting him also to 
 work with Clifford Curzon\, Igor Markevitch\, and Robert and Gaby Casades
 us\, among others. Yehudi Menuhin conducted the premiere of Naoumoff's fi
 rst piano concerto\, with him as soloist\, when Noumoff was 10 years old.
  Naoumoff pursued studies at the Paris Conservatory with Lelia Gousseau\,
  Pierre Sancan\, and Geneviève Joy\, as well as at the École Normale de
  Musique de Paris with Pierre Dervaux (conducting). Upon Boulanger's deat
 h\, he assumed her classes at the summer sessions of the Conservatoire d'
 Art Americain (Fontainebleau). He was later appointed to the Conservatoir
 e National Supérieur de Musique\, Paris. Naoumoff is regularly invited t
 o perform by the world's premier orchestras\, including the Los Angeles P
 hilharmonic\, Berlin Symphony\, and Vienna Symphony\, among many others. 
 He has worked closely with such conductors as Leonard Bernstein\, Leonard
  Slatkin\, and Mstislav Rostropovich. He has also collaborated with a hos
 t of noteworthy musicians\, including Jean-Pierre Rampal\, Gerard Souzay\
 , and Yo-Yo Ma. Naoumoff has been invited to perform at numerous music fe
 stivals\, such as the Santander Summer Masterclasses\, Verbier Academy Fe
 stival\, Banff Center\, and residencies at the Conservatory of Barcelona.
  He is an avid composer of French mélodies and is known for his transcri
 ptions for the piano. He maintains a video journal of daily improvisation
 s on his YouTube channel.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Repertoire</strong><br />  Beetho
 ven: <em>Sonata in F Minor\, Op. 2 No. 1: (1795)<br /></em>Chopin: <em>Ma
 zurka in F Minor\, Op. 68 No. 4 (1849)<br /></em>Chopin: <em>Waltz in C-S
 harp Minor\, Op. 64 No. 2 (1847)<br /></em>Chopin: <em>Nocturne in D-Flat
  Major\, Op. 27 No. 2 (1836)<br /></em>Chopin: <em>Prelude in -Flat Major
 \, Op. 28 No. 15 (c.1839)<br /></em>Chopin: <em>Etude in E-Flat Minor\, O
 p. 10 No. 6 (1832)<br /></em>Rachmaninoff: <em>Étude-tableau in E-Flat M
 inor\, Op. 39: No. 5 (1917)<br /></em>Scriabin: <em>Etude in C-Sharp Mino
 r\, Op. 2 No. 1 (1887)<br /></em>Brahms: <em>Intermezzo in A Major\, Op. 
 118 No. 2 (1893)<br /></em>Naoumoff: <em>Romance in D Minor (2012)<br /><
 /em><br /><strong>About the Artist</strong><br /><br /><strong>Émile</st
 rong><strong>Naoumoff</strong> is professor of music in piano at the Indi
 ana University Jacobs School of Music\, where he has been on faculty sinc
 e 1998. He has been likened to both Vladimir Horowitz and Arthur Rubinste
 in as a pianist\, displaying\, as one critic remarked\, the fire of the f
 ormer and the poetry of the latter. He revealed himself as a musical prod
 igy at age five\, playing the piano and adding composition to his studies
  a year later. At age seven\, he became the last disciple of Nadia Boulan
 ger\, who referred to him as "the gift of my old age." At age 18\, he was
  signed by music publisher Schott\, Mainz. Naoumoff studied with Boulange
 r until her death\, permitting him also to work with Clifford Curzon\, Ig
 or Markevitch\, and Robert and Gaby Casadesus\, among others. Yehudi Menu
 hin conducted the premiere of Naoumoff's first piano concerto\, with him 
 as soloist\, when Noumoff was 10 years old. Naoumoff pursued studies at t
 he Paris Conservatory with Lelia Gousseau\, Pierre Sancan\, and Genevièv
 e Joy\, as well as at the École Normale de Musique de Paris with Pierre 
 Dervaux (conducting). Upon Boulanger's death\, he assumed her classes at 
 the summer sessions of the Conservatoire d'Art Americain (Fontainebleau).
  He was later appointed to the Conservatoire National Supérieur de Musiq
 ue\, Paris. Naoumoff is regularly invited to perform by the world's premi
 er orchestras\, including the Los Angeles Philharmonic\, Berlin Symphony\
 , and Vienna Symphony\, among many others. He has worked closely with suc
 h conductors as Leonard Bernstein\, Leonard Slatkin\, and Mstislav Rostro
 povich. He has also collaborated with a host of noteworthy musicians\, in
 cluding Jean-Pierre Rampal\, Gerard Souzay\, and Yo-Yo Ma. Naoumoff has b
 een invited to perform at numerous music festivals\, such as the Santande
 r Summer Masterclasses\, Verbier Academy Festival\, Banff Center\, and re
 sidencies at the Conservatory of Barcelona. He is an avid composer of Fre
 nch mélodies and is known for his transcriptions for the piano. He maint
 ains a video journal of daily improvisations on his YouTube channel.</p>
