 s part of her Gold Medal prize at The Indianapolis. Passionate about cham
 ber music\, Huang will also perform chamber music concerts in Dallas and 
 Montreal. Huang is a recipient of numerous accolades and awards\, includi
 ng first prize at the Elmar Oliveira International Violin Competition (20
 17)\, winner of the New York Concert Artist Worldwide Debut Audition (201
 7)\, first prize gold medalist of the 6<sup>th</sup> International Tchaik
 ovsky Competition for Young Musicians (2009)\, and first prize and Audien
 ce Award at the Cooper International Competition (2011). That same year\,
  she was also named the first Artist-in-Residence of the Hartford Symphon
 y Orchestra. Huang made her solo debut with the National Taiwan Symphony 
 Orchestra in 2004 at the age of nine. She has since performed in 20 count
 ries across three continents with numerous prestigious ensembles\, includ
 ing the New York Philharmonic\, Cleveland Orchestra\, Baltimore\, Indiana
 polis\, Shanghai\, Russian\, Evergreen and Singapore symphony orchestras\
 , and the Staatskapelle Weimar in Germany. She has been a guest artist at
  the Verbier\, Marlboro\, Ravinia\, Aspen\, Newport\, Music in the Mounta
 ins\, and Eastern music festivals. Huang performs on a Guarneri del Gesù
  violin\, Cremona 1739 "Kortschak"\, on loan from Ryuji Ueno and Rare Vio
