BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20260630T233000Z
DTEND:20260701T013000Z
LOCATION:Auer Hall
SUMMARY:Gregory Sioles\, piano
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  Clementi: Sonata in F-Sharp Minor\, Op. 25 No. 5 
 (1790)Berio: Wasserklavier (1965)Chopin: Four Mazurkas\, Op. 41 (1839)Cho
 pin: Ballade No. 2 in F Major\, Op. 38 (1839)Mussorgsky: Pictures at an E
 xhibition (1874)About the Artist  American pianist Gregory Sioles is prai
 sed for passionate\, stylish\, and insightful performances. Sioles has be
 en the recipient of numerous honors and awards including a Fulbright Scho
 larship and an Atwater Kent Grand Prize. He has performed on three contin
 ents at such venues as the Purcell Room (London)\, Amerika Haus (Berlin)\
 , and the Shanghai Conservatory. Following his London debut\, he made sol
 o appearances in Spain\, Germany\, Switzerland\, and Scotland. In Spain a
 nd in London\, he served as the teaching assistant for the legendary peda
 gogue Maria Curcio. In addition to Curcio\, he studied with György Sebő
 k and Aube Tzerko. Sioles has performed throughout the United States as a
  recitalist and soloist with orchestra\, and been a featured artist on Na
 tional Public Radio. An avid chamber player\, he has collaborated with nu
 merous artists\, including Edgar Meyer\, Barry Tuckwell\, the Guarneri St
 ring Quartet\, and members of the Baltimore and National symphony orchest
 ras. As part of the Washington D.C. music scene for over a decade (1991-2
 003)\, he performed solo and chamber recitals in many of the city's impor
 tant halls\, including the Kennedy Center\, the National Gallery\, the Ph
 illips Collection\, the Cosmos Club\, Strathmore Hall\, and the French Em
 bassy. Recent international engagements have taken him to Taiwan\, where 
 he performed in five cities including Kaohsiung and Taipei\, and to Panam
 a and Costa Rica\, where he gave chamber and solo performances and taught
  master classes. From 2019 to 2024\, Sioles taught at the Indiana Univers
 ity Jacobs School of Music. Previously\, he taught at Louisiana State Uni
 versity\, the University of Maryland\, and Levine Music in Washington\, D
 .C. He records for Centaur Records.
